public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync {

    private static final Integer MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION = 100;

    public class CustomerPayload {
        @InvocableVariable
        public String salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;
    }

    /**
     * Callouts are not allowed directly from triggers, so the callouts are made
     * asynchronously after the transaction commits. One request is still sent per
     * new account, per the requirement that the ERP team can trace every account
     * on its own. A single future transaction is capped at 100 callouts, so large
     * marketplace batches (up to 200 accounts) are split into chunks of 100.
     */
    public static void syncNewAccounts(List<Account> newList) {
        if (newList == null || newList.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Future methods only support primitive/collection-of-primitive params,
        // so we build the JSON payload up front and pass it as a List<String>.
        List<String> chunk = new List<String>();
        for (Account acc : newList) {
            CustomerPayload p = new CustomerPayload();
            p.salesforceId = acc.Id; // 18-char Id in triggers
            p.name = acc.Name;
            p.country = String.isNotBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? acc.BillingCountry : null;
            chunk.add(JSON.serialize(p));

            if (chunk.size() == MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION) {
                sendChunk(chunk);
                chunk = new List<String>();
            }
        }
        if (!chunk.isEmpty()) {
            sendChunk(chunk);
        }
    }

    @future(callout=true)
    private static void sendChunk(List<String> customersJson) {
        for (String customerJson : customersJson) {
            CustomerPayload p = (CustomerPayload) JSON.deserialize(
                customerJson, CustomerPayload.class);

            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(new List<CustomerPayload>{ p }));

            new Http().send(req);
        }
    }
}
```

Note on deviations: the ticket asked for the callout "right from the trigger", but Salesforce does not allow synchronous callouts from triggers. The callouts run in a `@future(callout=true)` method queued after commit, still one request per account. Because future methods can't take non-primitive parameters, the per-account payload is serialized in the trigger context and passed as a `List<String>`, and batches over 100 accounts are split into multiple future transactions to respect the 100-callout-per-transaction limit.

