public with sharing class AccountRatingService {

    private static final Integer HOT_AMOUNT = 100000;

    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Accounts that have at least one open opportunity with Amount >= 100,000 -> Hot
        Set<Id> hotIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT AccountId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND IsClosed = false
              AND Amount >= :HOT_AMOUNT
        ]) {
            if (opp.AccountId != null) {
                hotIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // Accounts that have at least one open opportunity of any size -> Warm
        Set<Id> openIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT AccountId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND IsClosed = false
        ]) {
            if (opp.AccountId != null) {
                openIds.add(opp.AccountId);
            }
        }

        List<Account> updates = new List<Account>();
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            String rating = hotIds.contains(accountId)
                ? 'Hot'
                : (openIds.contains(accountId) ? 'Warm' : 'Cold');
            updates.add(new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = rating));
        }

        if (!updates.isEmpty()) {
            update updates;
        }
    }
}
```

## What changed and why

1. **One future call per trigger chunk, not per opportunity.** The failing test proved the point I flagged earlier: 200 opportunities = up to 200 future calls, but a transaction allows only 50. The trigger now collects the **distinct** `AccountId`s and makes a single future call with the whole set. The method signature changed from `recalculate(Id)` to `recalculate(Set<Id>)` — this is a deliberate, necessary deviation from the "one future call per opportunity" instruction; the per-opportunity approach cannot survive a 200-record CPQ batch.

2. **Bulkified service.** The future method now queries in bulk (`WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds`), classifies accounts into Hot/Warm/Cold, and updates all accounts in a single DML — so it also scales to the 10,000-account future-context DML limit rather than one query+DML per account.

3. Everything else stands: recalculating on close-to-closed stage transitions, using the standard `IsClosed` field instead of assuming custom open stage names, skipping null `AccountId`, and null `Amount` never qualifying as Hot.

I inlined the dispatch logic into the trigger (removing the dependency on a third class) and fixed the `subList` issue (which doesn't exist on Apex `List`).

