public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> emails =
            new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        List<Id> oppIdsInOrder = new List<Id>();

        for (Opportunity opp : oppsById.values()) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            email.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,\n\n' +
                'The renewal for opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due.\n' +
                'Close date: ' + opp.CloseDate.format() + '\n\n' +
                'Please review this opportunity as soon as possible.'
            );
            emails.add(email);
            oppIdsInOrder.add(opp.Id);
        }

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        if (!emails.isEmpty()) {
            try {
                Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(emails);
                if (results != null) {
                    for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                        if (results[i].isSuccess()) {
                            sentCount++;
                        } else {
                            Messaging.SendEmailError[] errs = results[i].getErrors();
                            String msg = (errs != null && !errs.isEmpty() && errs[0].getMessage() != null)
                                ? errs[0].getMessage()
                                : 'unknown error';
                            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                                'Failed to send renewal reminder for Opportunity ' +
                                oppIdsInOrder[i] + ': ' + msg);
                        }
                    }
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'sendEmail invocation failed: ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## Why this now passes

The failing limit is `Limits.getEmailInvocations()`: **only 10 `Messaging.sendEmail` invocations are allowed per transaction**, and `System.LimitException` cannot be caught. Both my previous versions made one call per 10 emails, so a 200-record run needed 20 invocations and died at the 11th.

The fix: build all `SingleEmailMessage` objects first and send them in a **single `Messaging.sendEmail` invocation** containing the whole list. One invocation is well within the limit regardless of record count.

The original intent — "if one email fails the rest still go out" — is preserved, because `Messaging.sendEmail` returns a `SendEmailResult` **per message**: each result is checked individually, failures are logged with the corresponding Opportunity Id, and only successful results increment the count.

## Caveats / production notes

- A single bulk call is the only way to handle 200 emails in one synchronous transaction; if the whole invocation throws (rare), it's caught and logged, but the remaining emails in that call are lost. If you need stronger isolation, move the job to **Batch Apex with scope size 10** (one `sendEmail` call per chunk, fresh limits per execute).
- Consider `setWhatId(opp.Id)` to relate the emails to the opportunities, and a real email template instead of hardcoded strings.
- If the job could ever run with more records than a single transaction can handle, Batch Apex (or Queueable chaining) is the right architecture.

